🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗥𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗯𝗮𝗰𝗸 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗼𝘂𝗹𝗱 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗥𝗲𝗰𝗼𝘃𝗲𝗿 𝗙𝗿𝗼𝗺 𝗮 𝗙𝗮𝗶𝗹𝗲𝗱 𝗣𝗿𝗼𝗱𝘂𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻 𝗗𝗲𝗽𝗹𝗼𝘆𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “You deployed a new application version to production, but users started experiencing errors immediately. How will you recover?”

In production, the priority is to restore service safely while understanding what went wrong.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗮𝗰𝗵

1️⃣ Confirm the impact

Identify affected services, users, error rates and when the issue started.

2️⃣ Check the rollout

kubectl rollout status deployment/<deployment-name> -n <namespace>
kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

Look for failed Pods, readiness issues and restart counts.

3️⃣ Check application logs

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>
kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace> --previous

Investigate errors, failed dependencies and configuration problems.

4️⃣ Review deployment history

kubectl rollout history deployment/<deployment-name> -n <namespace>

Compare the current release with the previous working revision.

5️⃣ Roll back if necessary

If the new release is confirmed as the cause and rollback is approved:

kubectl rollout undo deployment/<deployment-name> -n <namespace>

6️⃣ Verify recovery

kubectl rollout status deployment/<deployment-name> -n <namespace>
kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

Also verify application health, error rates and user-facing functionality.

7️⃣ Perform Root Cause Analysis (RCA)

Investigate the failed change, document the incident and define preventive actions before the next deployment.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

Rollback restores a previous version; it does not automatically fix the root cause.

Always follow the approved recovery process and verify the application after rollback.
